An emergency nosebleed, often called epistaxis, can be identified by severe, uncontrollable bleeding, difficulty breathing due to blood obstruction, or heavy blood loss causing dizziness or fainting. If the bleeding persists for more than 20 minutes despite attempts to control it, or if the person is very young, elderly, or has an underlying health condition, it should be considered an emergency. In addition, if the nosebleed occurs after a head injury or is accompanied by other symptoms like vomiting or severe headache, immediate medical attention is necessary.
